LEARN.course({id:"bossa-nova",family:"Jazz",name:"Bossa nova",tagline:"A whispered samba: thumb bass, syncopated finger chords, color-tone harmony and a voice at speaking volume.",level:"Intermediate",era:"Late 1950s to today",artists:["João Gilberto","Antônio Carlos Jobim","Vinicius de Moraes","Stan Getz","Astrud Gilberto","Elis Regina","Luiz Bonfá"],listen:["João Gilberto – Chega de Saudade","João Gilberto – Desafinado","Stan Getz & João Gilberto – Getz/Gilberto (album)","Antônio Carlos Jobim – Wave (album)","Elis Regina & Antônio Carlos Jobim – Águas de Março"],key:"F major",bpm:66,meter:"4/4",techniques:["fingerstyle-pima","jazz-voicings","chord-embellishments"],sources:[{id:"s1",title:"João Gilberto",site:"Encyclopaedia Britannica",url:"https://www.britannica.com/biography/Joao-Gilberto",kind:"encyclopedia"},{id:"s2",title:"Bossa nova",site:"Wikipedia",url:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bossa_nova",kind:"encyclopedia"},{id:"s3",title:"João Gilberto",site:"Wikipedia",url:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jo%C3%A3o_Gilberto",kind:"encyclopedia"},{id:"s4",title:"Antônio Carlos Jobim",site:"Wikipedia",url:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ant%C3%B4nio_Carlos_Jobim",kind:"encyclopedia"},{id:"s5",title:"Bossa Nova Guitar",author:"Larry Gray",site:"Jazz Guitar @ Illinois (University of Illinois)",year:2017,url:"https://publish.illinois.edu/illinoisjazzguitar/2017/10/22/bossa-nova-guitar/",kind:"lesson"},{id:"s6",title:"Chromatic Devices in the Songs of Antônio Carlos Jobim",author:"Simon Cosgrove",site:"Joshibi University of Art and Design repository",url:"https://joshibi.repo.nii.ac.jp/records/11.1",kind:"paper"},{id:"s7",title:"The Jobim Chord Progression",author:"John Proulx and Michael LaDisa",site:"PianoWithJonny",url:"https://pianowithjonny.com/piano-lessons/the-jobim-chord-progression/",kind:"lesson"},{id:"s8",title:"How to Play a Bossa Nova Groove on the Drum Set",author:"Julius Epic",site:"B+C Guides",url:"https://guides.brit.co/guides/play-a-bossa-nova-groove-on-the-drum-set",kind:"lesson"},{id:"s9",title:"Classical guitar",site:"Wikipedia",url:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Classical_guitar",kind:"encyclopedia"},{id:"s11",title:"Thirty-two-bar form",site:"Wikipedia",url:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thirty-two-bar_form",kind:"encyclopedia"},{id:"s12",title:"João Gilberto, Pioneer Of Brazilian Bossa Nova, Has Died At 88",author:"Ana Monroy Yglesias",site:"GRAMMY.com (Recording Academy)",year:2019,url:"https://www.grammy.com/news/joao-gilberto-pioneer-brazilian-bossa-nova-has-died-88",kind:"article"},{id:"s13",title:"João Gilberto, father of bossa nova, dead at 88",author:"Daniel Ong",site:"Guitar.com",year:2019,url:"https://guitar.com/news/music-news/joao-gilberto-bossa-nova-dead-88/",kind:"article"},{id:"s14",title:"How to mic an acoustic guitar",author:"Bo Brinck",site:"DPA Microphones (Mic University)",url:"https://www.dpamicrophones.com/mic-university/how-to-mic/how-to-mic-an-acoustic-guitar/",kind:"lesson"}],study:[{title:"Chega de Saudade",kind:"album",author:"João Gilberto (1959)",note:"The record that set the template. Listen to the thumb and the fingers as two separate instruments."},{title:"Getz/Gilberto",kind:"album",author:"Stan Getz and João Gilberto (1964)",note:"Hear the batida hold steady under a jazz soloist and a soft vocal at the same time."},{title:"Elis & Tom",kind:"album",author:"Elis Regina and Antônio Carlos Jobim (1974)",note:"Jobim songs with a fuller band, good for hearing color chords in context."},{title:"The Music of Antônio Carlos Jobim",kind:"book",author:"Peter Freeman",url:"https://www.intellectbooks.com/the-music-of-antonio-carlos-jobim",note:"A study of Jobim's harmonic language, including his descending chromatic lines and extended chords."},{title:"The Song Style of Antonio Carlos Jobim: An Analysis of Four Songs",kind:"paper",author:"Rachel Rogers",url:"https://aquila.usm.edu/masters_theses/596",note:"A close song-by-song analysis, most useful once you can play the progressions in this course."},{title:"Bossa Nova Drum Beats",kind:"lesson",author:"Drumeo",url:"https://freedrumlessons.com/drum-lessons/bossa-nova-beats.php",note:"Four bossa beats plus a PDF, handy when you want to program variations of the groove."},{title:"Intro to Bossa Nova Comping",kind:"lesson",author:"JazzGuitarLessons.net",url:"https://www.jazzguitarlessons.net/blog/intro-bossa-nova-comping",note:"More comping patterns to vary the batida once the basic one is solid."}],steps:[{id:"sound",title:"The sound: samba, softly",body:"Bossa nova grew up in Rio de Janeiro in the late 1950s as a relaxed style of samba. Britannica calls João Gilberto's 1958 single of Antônio Carlos Jobim's \"Chega de Saudade\" widely recognized as the first bossa nova single, and the 1959 LP of the same name took Brazil by storm.\n\nThree things define the sound. Gilberto's nylon-string guitar boils samba's percussion down to one player: the thumb stylizes the surdo drum and the fingers phrase like a tamborim. Jobim's songs bring sophisticated harmony shaped by jazz and by Debussy and Ravel. And the voice sits close to the volume of ordinary speech.\n\nThe style reached the rest of the world through *Getz/Gilberto*, which became the first jazz album to win the Grammy for Album of the Year. Many bossa songs are now jazz standards, but the feel stays Brazilian: bossa is typically played straight, without jazz swing.",cites:["s1","s2","s3","s4","s12","s13"],blocks:[{type:"callout",tone:"history",text:"Before bossa nova, Brazilian singers often used brassy, almost operatic styles. Gilberto's laid-back, understated delivery was one of the key changes, and it still defines the style.",cites:["s2","s3","s12"]}]},{id:"harmony",title:"Jobim's harmony: color chords and chromatic lines",body:"Jobim was an innovator in bringing sophisticated harmony into popular song. In this course nearly every chord carries a color tone: major sevenths and 6/9 chords at home, minor ninths on ii, and dominants with a flat nine that pull toward their target. Jobim's melodies often insist on the major seventh of the chord, so the color tone becomes the tune.\n\nTwo moves are worth learning first. **The II7.** In a major key, the second chord is often a dominant (G7 in F) held for two bars before it relaxes into ii7 and V7. Some players hear it as V of V with a delayed resolution, others as a Lydian sound. **The chromatic line.** Simon Cosgrove's study of 40 Jobim songs found descending chromatic bass lines (four or more semitones in a row) in 18 of them, and it also tracks inner voices that slide by half steps.\n\nOn nylon, use four-note voicings with the root on the sixth or fifth string, so the thumb has its own note and the fingers take the top three.",cites:["s4","s6","s7"],blocks:[{type:"progression",name:"A section with a II7",chords:"Imaj7 II7 ii7 V7b9 Imaj7 II7 ii7 bII7",note:"In F: Fmaj7, G7, Gm7, C7b9, then the same again with Gb7 sliding down a half step into the next Fmaj7.",cites:["s7"]},{type:"progression",name:"Chromatic bass walk",chords:"I6/9 viiø7 bVII9 VI7b9 ii9 bII7 Imaj7 -",note:"The bass falls F, E, Eb, D, then G, Gb, F: two chromatic descents under color chords.",cites:["s6"]},{type:"shapes",title:"Nylon voicings in F",shapes:[{name:"Fmaj7",frets:"1 x 2 2 1 x"},{name:"F6/9",frets:"x 8 7 7 8 x"},{name:"G7",frets:"3 x 3 4 3 x"},{name:"Gm7",frets:"3 x 3 3 3 x"},{name:"Gm9",frets:"3 x 3 3 3 5"},{name:"Gb7",frets:"2 x 2 3 2 x"},{name:"C7b9",frets:"x 3 2 3 2 x"},{name:"D7b9",frets:"x 5 4 5 4 x"},{name:"Em7b5",frets:"x 7 8 7 8 x"},{name:"Eb9",frets:"x 6 5 6 6 x"}],note:"The lowest note is the thumb's. Index, middle and ring take the three notes above it."},{type:"technique",id:"jazz-voicings",note:"Bossa uses the same shell-plus-color idea as jazz comping, with the root kept low for the thumb."},{type:"technique",id:"chord-embellishments",note:"Add the 9th, 6th or flat 9 to plain chords until every bar has one color tone."}]},{id:"rhythm",title:"The batida and the bossa groove",body:"Gilberto's right hand works like a small samba band. The thumb keeps a steady pulse in the bass, like the surdo. The index, middle and ring fingers pluck the top of the chord together in syncopated spots, like the tamborim. Counted in 4/4, the thumb plays half notes on beats 1 and 3, and the most basic finger pattern lands on 1, 2 and the and of 3. On \"The Girl from Ipanema\" Gilberto starts with a one-bar pattern and grows it into a two-bar pattern.\n\nKeep the chords short and even. The thumb never pushes; the fingers carry the syncopation.\n\nOn a drum kit, the samba parts adapt easily: the bass drum takes the surdo, a cross-stick on the snare plays a two-bar clave figure, and the hi-hat keeps steady eighths, quieter than the rim clicks. Fretkit's song map counts bossa in a slow four, so one bar of the groove holds both bars of the clave. At 66 bpm it feels like 132 in cut time, the tempo of the tabs.",cites:["s2","s5","s8"],blocks:[{type:"tab",id:"bossa-nova-batida-basic",title:"Basic batida on Fmaj7 and G7",instr:"guitar",bpm:120,grid:2,tone:"bossa-nova-nylon",notes:[[0,6,1,4,"P"],[0,4,2,2,"I"],[0,3,2,2,"M"],[0,2,1,2,"A"],[2,4,2,2,"I"],[2,3,2,2,"M"],[2,2,1,2,"A"],[4,6,1,4,"P"],[5,4,2,2,"I"],[5,3,2,2,"M"],[5,2,1,2,"A"],[8,6,1,4,"P"],[8,4,2,2,"I"],[8,3,2,2,"M"],[8,2,1,2,"A"],[10,4,2,2,"I"],[10,3,2,2,"M"],[10,2,1,2,"A"],[12,6,1,4,"P"],[13,4,2,2,"I"],[13,3,2,2,"M"],[13,2,1,2,"A"],[16,6,3,4,"P"],[16,4,3,2,"I"],[16,3,4,2,"M"],[16,2,3,2,"A"],[18,4,3,2,"I"],[18,3,4,2,"M"],[18,2,3,2,"A"],[20,6,3,4,"P"],[21,4,3,2,"I"],[21,3,4,2,"M"],[21,2,3,2,"A"],[24,6,3,4,"P"],[24,4,3,2,"I"],[24,3,4,2,"M"],[24,2,3,2,"A"],[26,4,3,2,"I"],[26,3,4,2,"M"],[26,2,3,2,"A"],[28,6,3,4,"P"],[29,4,3,2,"I"],[29,3,4,2,"M"],[29,2,3,2,"A"]],note:"Thumb (P) on beats 1 and 3, fingers (I, M, A) together on 1, 2 and the and of 3. Start at half speed and keep the thumb dead even."},{type:"tab",id:"bossa-nova-batida-two-bar",title:"Two-bar batida through a chromatic ii–V",instr:"guitar",bpm:120,grid:2,tone:"bossa-nova-nylon",notes:[[0,6,3,4,"P"],[0,4,3,2,"I"],[0,3,3,2,"M"],[0,2,3,2,"A"],[3,4,3,2,"I"],[3,3,3,2,"M"],[3,2,3,2,"A"],[4,6,3,4,"P"],[6,4,3,2,"I"],[6,3,3,2,"M"],[6,2,3,2,"A"],[8,6,2,4,"P"],[10,4,2,2,"I"],[10,3,3,2,"M"],[10,2,2,2,"A"],[12,6,2,4,"P"],[13,4,2,2,"I"],[13,3,3,2,"M"],[13,2,2,2,"A"],[16,6,1,4,"P"],[16,4,2,2,"I"],[16,3,2,2,"M"],[16,2,1,2,"A"],[19,4,2,2,"I"],[19,3,2,2,"M"],[19,2,1,2,"A"],[20,6,1,4,"P"],[22,4,2,2,"I"],[22,3,2,2,"M"],[22,2,1,2,"A"],[24,6,1,4,"P"],[26,4,2,2,"I"],[26,3,2,2,"M"],[26,2,1,2,"A"],[28,6,1,4,"P"],[29,4,2,2,"I"],[29,3,2,2,"M"],[29,2,1,2,"A"]],note:"Gm7, Gb7, Fmaj7. The fingers now follow the drum clave: 1, and of 2, 4, then 2, and of 3. Listen for the inner voices sliding down by half steps."},{type:"technique",id:"fingerstyle-pima",note:"Bossa is p-i-m-a in its simplest form: the thumb alone on the bass, three fingers plucking together."},{type:"groove",groove:{id:"bossa-nova-xstick",name:"Bossa cross-stick",role:"Verse",steps:16,beats:4,meter:"4/4",swing:0,t:{hh:"xgxgxgxgxgxgxgxg",rm:"x..x..x...x..x..",kk:"x..xx..xx..xx..x"}},note:"Each step is an eighth note of the fast count. Cross-stick on 1, and of 2, 4, then 2, and of 3; the kick plays the surdo figure on 1, and of 2, 3, and of 4.",cites:["s8"]},{type:"groove",groove:{id:"bossa-nova-ride",name:"Bossa on the ride",role:"Chorus",steps:16,beats:4,meter:"4/4",swing:0,t:{rd:"xgxgxgxgxgxgxgxg",rm:"x..x..x...x..x..",kk:"x..xx..xx..xx..x"}},note:"The same clave and kick with the eighths moved to the ride, for a lift in the bridge."}]},{id:"melody",title:"Melody and a quiet voice",body:'Bossa singing is laid-back and understated. Gilberto was known for whispery vocals at a volume close to ordinary speech, and he used one microphone for the voice and another for the guitar so the two could compete equally. The voice moved into the room with the listener.\n\nJobim\'s melodies often insist on the major seventh of the chord. The phrase below works the same way: it moves in small steps, holds color notes, and starts some notes just before the bar line so they hang across it. Write lines you could hum at conversation volume.\n\nThe lyric themes are close and personal: women, love, longing, homesickness and nature. The phrase below models a vocal line over the A section. Play it softly with alternating index and middle fingers, then sing it on "da" before you put words to your own.',cites:["s2","s3","s4","s13"],blocks:[{type:"tab",id:"bossa-nova-melody",title:"Vocal-style phrase over Fmaj7, G7, Gm7, C7b9",instr:"guitar",bpm:132,grid:2,tone:"bossa-nova-nylon",notes:[[1,2,8,1,"I"],[2,1,5,1,"M"],[3,2,8,1,"I"],[4,2,5,3,"M"],[7,1,5,3,"I"],[10,1,7,1,"M"],[11,1,5,1,"I"],[12,2,5,3,"M"],[15,1,10,3,"I"],[18,1,8,1,"M"],[19,1,6,1,"I"],[20,1,5,2,"M"],[22,2,6,1,"I"],[23,3,6,3,"M"],[26,2,5,1,"I"],[27,2,8,1,"M"],[28,1,6,2,"I"],[30,1,5,2,"M"]],note:"The phrase holds the major seventh (E) over Fmaj7, the 9th and 13th over G7, and anticipates each new bar by an eighth note. The Db at the end of bar 3 is the flat nine of C7b9."},{type:"lyrics",section:"verse",prompt:"Write a quiet first verse set in one small, bright place: a window over the street, a path to the sea, a café table at dusk. Let one image from nature carry your longing for someone who is not there.",tips:["Bossa lyrics stay close to love, longing, homesickness and nature. Pick one and keep the scene small.","Write words you could say at conversation volume: short words, soft consonants, no shouting vowels.","End lines on open vowels you can hold softly, because the melody often hangs on a held color note.","Name the light, the water or the season, and let the listener feel the feeling without you naming it."],cites:["s2","s3"]},{type:"callout",tone:"tip",text:"Sing every phrase before you play it. If you cannot sing it quietly, the line is too busy for this style."}]},{id:"tone",title:"Nylon tone up close",body:"Bossa nova is most commonly played on a nylon-string classical guitar, with the fingers. The target is round and close: a warm thumb, soft chords and no edge.\n\nMost of the tone comes from your right hand. Players shape their nails for a brighter tone and feel; the flesh of the finger gives a softer sound. Where you pluck matters as much: toward the fingerboard the tone gets warmer, toward the bridge it turns more metallic.\n\nFor recording, a good starting point is a microphone a short distance from the guitar, aimed between the fretboard and the sound hole. Moving a directional mic closer adds low end from the proximity effect, so back it off a little or trim the low end so the thumb stays clear. If you sing too, give the voice its own mic, as Gilberto did, so neither part has to push.",cites:["s2","s3","s9","s13","s14"],blocks:[{type:"tone",id:"bossa-nova-nylon",name:"Close, warm nylon",kind:"nylon",guitar:"Nylon-string classical guitar",pickups:"Close microphone (or an undersaddle pickup on stage)",strings:"Normal-tension nylon",amp:"Microphone into a clean preamp, or a clean acoustic amp live",settings:{gain:1,bass:4,mid:5,treble:4,presence:3,reverb:3},pedals:["eq","reverb"],demo:"bossa-nova-batida-basic",tips:["Play with the fingers. Bossa is most often played on nylon strings without a pick.","Use the flesh of your thumb for a round bass note. Nails brighten the attack, so let the fingers decide how much edge the chords get.","Pluck over the end of the fingerboard for a warmer tone. Moving toward the bridge makes it more metallic.","Start with the mic between the fretboard and the sound hole. If a directional mic close in adds boom, cut a little low end (the EQ pedal here).","Sing and play at speaking volume. Gilberto used separate mics for voice and guitar so the two could sit side by side."],cites:["s2","s3","s9","s14"],note:"Turn the bass and gain down before you turn anything up. Bossa tone is mostly subtraction."}]},{id:"form",title:"Song form: AABA, softly",body:"Many bossa songs joined the jazz standard repertoire, where the 32-bar AABA shape is common: four eight-bar sections, with the B section (the bridge) bringing contrasting harmony or feel. Bossa uses it gently. The A sections circle the home chord through color chords and chromatic slides, and the bridge moves somewhere warmer before it slides home.\n\nThe map below has an intro, three A sections and a bridge that leans on the IV chord. Its minor version, Bbm6, slips the D of Bbmaj7 down to Db inside the chord: the kind of half-step inner motion Cosgrove traces through Jobim's songs. This map leaves fills out so the groove stays light. Switch the cross-stick groove to the ride for the bridge and come back for the last A.",cites:["s2","s6","s11"],blocks:[{type:"progression",name:"Bridge: IV, minor iv, home",chords:"IVmaj7 - iv6 bVII9 iii7 VI7b9 ii9 V7b9",note:"Bbmaj7, Bbm6, Eb9, then Am7, D7b9, Gm9, C7b9 to lead back to Fmaj7.",cites:["s6"]},{type:"form",name:"Bossa song map",sections:[{type:"intro",bars:4,chords:"ii9 bII7 Imaj7 -",groove:"bossa-nova-xstick",fill:""},{type:"verse",name:"A1",bars:8,chords:"Imaj7 II7 ii7 V7b9 Imaj7 II7 ii7 bII7",groove:"bossa-nova-xstick",fill:""},{type:"verse",name:"A2",bars:8,chords:"I6/9 viiø7 bVII9 VI7b9 ii9 bII7 Imaj7 -",groove:"bossa-nova-xstick",fill:""},{type:"bridge",name:"B",bars:8,chords:"IVmaj7 - iv6 bVII9 iii7 VI7b9 ii9 V7b9",groove:"bossa-nova-ride",fill:""},{type:"verse",name:"A3",bars:8,chords:"Imaj7 II7 ii7 V7b9 Imaj7 II7 ii7 bII7",groove:"bossa-nova-xstick",fill:""},{type:"outro",bars:4,chords:"ii9 bII7 I6/9 -",groove:"bossa-nova-xstick",fill:""}],note:"Each bar of this map holds two bars of the guitar pattern. Write the lyric for A1 first, then let A2 and A3 answer it."}]},{id:"together",title:"Put it together",body:"Build your song from the bottom up. Get the batida steady on one chord, then walk it through the A section until the thumb no longer notices the chord changes. Load the song map, set the cross-stick groove, and play the progression with the drums at a slow tempo.\n\nThen sing. Hum your melody over the A section at speaking volume, find the color note it wants to hold, and write your verse around that note. Record a take with the mic close and the gain low, and listen back for anything that sounds pushed.",cites:["s2","s3","s5"],blocks:[{type:"checklist",items:["Your thumb plays steady half notes on 1 and 3 while the fingers pluck short chords on 1, 2 and the and of 3.","You can switch to the two-bar pattern without the thumb wavering.","Every chord in your progression carries a color tone: maj7, 6/9, m9 or 7b9.","Your song has at least one chromatic slide: a half-step dominant or a falling bass line.","The drums are a quiet cross-stick clave with soft hats and kick.","You sing at speaking volume and your melody leans on a held color note.","Your lyric stays with one small scene of love, longing, home or nature."]}]}]}),LEARN.course({id:"modal-jazz",family:"Jazz",name:"Cool and modal jazz",tagline:"Long stretches on one mode, open fourth-stacked chords, and solos built from space and small ideas.",level:"Intermediate",era:"Late 1950s to today",artists:["Miles Davis","Bill Evans","John Coltrane","Grant Green","Kenny Burrell","Jim Hall","John Scofield","Pat Martino"],listen:["Miles Davis – So What","Miles Davis – Flamenco Sketches","John Coltrane – Impressions","Herbie Hancock – Maiden Voyage","Grant Green – Solid (album)","Bill Evans & Jim Hall – Undercurrent (album)"],key:"D minor",bpm:136,meter:"4/4",techniques:["jazz-voicings","chord-embellishments","alternate-picking","walking-bass"],sources:[{id:"m1",title:"Modal jazz",site:"Wikipedia",url:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Modal_jazz",kind:"encyclopedia"},{id:"m2",title:"Kind of Blue",site:"Wikipedia",url:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kind_of_Blue",kind:"encyclopedia"},{id:"m6",title:"A Guitarist Who Can Build Jazz on Silence",site:"The New York Times",year:1995,url:"https://www.nytimes.com/1995/09/26/arts/a-guitarist-who-can-build-jazz-on-silence.html",kind:"article"},{id:"m8",title:"Pat Martino",site:"Wikipedia",url:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pat_Martino",kind:"encyclopedia"},{id:"m9",title:"Jazz guitar",site:"Wikipedia",url:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jazz_guitar",kind:"encyclopedia"},{id:"m12",title:"Quartal Chords: Harmony and Voicings for Guitar",site:"JazzGuitar.be",url:"https://www.jazzguitar.be/blog/quartal-chords-harmony-voicings-for-guitar/",kind:"lesson"},{id:"m13",title:"What Are Modes In Music? 7 Music Modes Explained",author:"James Melton",site:"Learn Jazz Standards",year:2024,url:"https://www.learnjazzstandards.com/blog/deepmodes/",kind:"lesson"},{id:"m14",title:"So What Chord: Jazz Rhythm Guitar Lesson (Modal Comping)",author:"Stef Ramin",site:"Jazz Guitar Licks",year:2017,url:"https://jazz-guitar-licks.com/blog/so-what-chord-jazz-guitar-rhythm-comping-lesson.html",kind:"lesson"},{id:"m15",title:"Digging Deeper: Jazz Piano Chords for Guitar",author:"Arthur Rotfeld",site:"Premier Guitar",year:2018,url:"https://www.premierguitar.com/articles/27273-digging-deeper-jazz-piano-chords-for-guitar",kind:"lesson"},{id:"m16",title:"Jim Hall",site:"National Endowment for the Arts (NEA Jazz Masters)",url:"https://www.arts.gov/honors/jazz/jim-hall",kind:"article"},{id:"m17",title:"Jim Hall Dies",author:"Crystal Larsen",site:"GRAMMY.com (Recording Academy)",url:"https://www.grammy.com/news/jim-hall-dies",kind:"article"},{id:"m18",title:"Grant Green",site:"Blue Note Records",url:"https://www.bluenote.com/artist/grant-green/",kind:"article"}],study:[{title:"Kind of Blue",kind:"album",author:"Miles Davis (1959)",note:"The core text. Listen to how little the chords move and how much the melodies and voicings do."},{title:"Solid",kind:"album",author:"Grant Green (recorded 1964)",note:"Green's horn-like single lines over McCoy Tyner and Elvin Jones from Coltrane's quartet."},{title:"Undercurrent",kind:"album",author:"Bill Evans and Jim Hall (1962)",note:"Two players leaving room for each other: a lesson in space and voicing for any guitarist."},{title:"The Jazz Piano Book",kind:"book",author:"Mark Levine (1989)",note:"A standard reference for So What and fourth-based voicings; the ideas move straight to guitar."},{title:"Modal Jazz Composition & Harmony, Vol. 1",kind:"book",author:"Ron Miller (Advance Music, 1996)",note:"A composer's view of modal harmony, useful when you start writing your own vamps."},{title:"Lydian Chromatic Concept of Tonal Organization",kind:"book",author:"George Russell",url:"https://www.georgerussell.com/",note:"The theory that pushed Davis, Coltrane and others toward modes. Dense, but it explains where the idea came from."},{title:"Smithsonian Jazz Oral History: Jim Hall (transcript)",kind:"interview",url:"https://www.si.edu/media/NMAH/NMAH-AC0808_Hall_Jim_Transcript.pdf",note:"Hall in his own words about his life and his way of playing."}],steps:[{id:"sound",title:"The sound: fewer chords, more melody",body:'By the 1950s, bebop and hard bop soloists improvised over fast, repeating chord progressions. Modal jazz pulled the other way. Pieces were organized around modes instead of conventional chord changes, so one scale could last for many bars. George Russell\'s experiments encouraged players such as Miles Davis and Bill Evans in this direction, and Davis saw modal writing as a way back to melody.\n\nThe landmarks are Davis\'s "Milestones" (1958) and *Kind of Blue* (1959), where he gave the players scales instead of chord charts. "So What" spends sixteen bars on one mode, eight on another and eight back on the first. "Flamenco Sketches" moves through five scales, each played as long as the soloist wishes.\n\nGuitarists found their own way in. Grant Green, who became a Blue Note star in the early 1960s, played single-note lines and recorded with McCoy Tyner and Elvin Jones from Coltrane\'s quartet. Kenny Burrell was among the postwar small-group players who made the guitar a serious jazz instrument. Jim Hall made silence part of his music, and John Scofield, Bill Frisell and Pat Metheny have all cited him as an influence.',cites:["m1","m2","m6","m9","m17","m18"],blocks:[{type:"callout",tone:"history",text:"Not every great player over a vamp thinks in modes. Pat Martino built his system on major and minor third intervals and said of modal thinking that he had never operated that way. Learn the modes, then let your ear lead.",cites:["m8"]}]},{id:"harmony",title:"Dorian, Lydian and the So What voicing",body:'The home sound of modal jazz is **Dorian**: natural minor with a major sixth. In D Dorian that sixth is B, and it keeps a minor chord sounding open. **Lydian** is the major scale with a raised fourth; over Fmaj7 the raised fourth is also B, and it gives the floating major color.\n\nOn a chord that does not move, the voicings have to create the motion. On "So What" Bill Evans made famous a five-note chord built from three perfect fourths and a major third, now called the So What chord. On guitar it falls right under the hand: the five lowest open strings, E A D G B, are a So What chord on E. Moved to D (x 5 5 5 6 5) it gives a Dm11 sound.\n\nEvans was among the first pianists to fully embrace rootless voicings: he left the root to the bassist and often played a triad with an added note over the bass line. McCoy Tyner went further and stacked fourths within a mode. Do the same. Let the bass own the D, and move fourth-stacked shapes up and down the mode. The chord stays the same; the color shifts.',cites:["m12","m13","m14","m15"],blocks:[{type:"progression",name:"Dorian vamp",chords:"i11 - - - - - - -",note:"Eight bars of Dm11. All the motion comes from voicings inside D Dorian over a steady D in the bass.",cites:["m13"]},{type:"progression",name:"Half-step lift",chords:"i11 - - - bii11 - - -",note:'Dm11 to Ebm11: the same shape one fret higher, the move behind the B sections of "So What" and "Impressions".',cites:["m1","m14"]},{type:"progression",name:"Lydian float",key:"F major",chords:"Imaj7#11 - II -",note:"Fmaj7#11, then a G major triad with F kept in the bass (G over F). Both chords come from F Lydian.",cites:["m13"]},{type:"shapes",title:"Fourth-stacked shapes in D Dorian",shapes:[{name:"So What on D (Dm11)",frets:"x 5 5 5 6 5"},{name:"So What on Eb (Ebm11)",frets:"x 6 6 6 7 6"},{name:"So What on E (open)",frets:"0 0 0 0 0 x"},{name:"Fourths from D",frets:"x 5 5 5 6 x"},{name:"Fourths from E",frets:"x 7 7 7 8 x"},{name:"Fourths from A",frets:"x 12 12 12 13 x"},{name:"Rootless Dm9",frets:"x x 3 2 1 0"},{name:"Fmaj7#11",frets:"1 x 2 2 0 x"}],note:'The four-note "fourths" shapes stack three perfect fourths, and every note stays inside D Dorian.',cites:["m12","m14"]},{type:"scale",root:"D",scale:"dorian",view:"box",note:"Find the B (the major sixth) in every position. It is the note that says Dorian."},{type:"technique",id:"jazz-voicings",note:"Shell voicings still work here; quartal shapes are the modal extension of the same idea."},{type:"technique",id:"chord-embellishments",note:"On a static chord, move one inner note at a time (9 to 11 and back) to keep the comping alive."}]},{id:"rhythm",title:"Rhythm: swing over a still chord",body:"With so few chord changes, the rhythm section carries the motion. On *Kind of Blue* that meant Paul Chambers on bass and Jimmy Cobb on drums. In Coltrane's quartet, Elvin Jones on drums with Reggie Workman and later Jimmy Garrison on bass pushed modal playing further, with McCoy Tyner's piano on top.\n\nUnder a vamp, a walking bass line (card below) gives the chord its forward motion. The two grooves here put the swing on the ride cymbal with the hi-hat on 2 and 4. Use the lighter two-feel for the head and the busier groove, with snare comping, for the solos.\n\nOn guitar, comp in short stabs and leave holes. The pattern below uses five fourth-stacked shapes from D Dorian. It mixes the Charleston rhythm, a stab on beat 1 and a short one on the *and* of 2, with shapes that climb on the beat. Listen to how the color moves while the chord name never changes.",cites:["m1","m2","m14"],blocks:[{type:"groove",groove:{id:"modal-jazz-two",name:"Modal two-feel",role:"Verse",steps:12,beats:4,meter:"4/4",t:{rd:"x..x.xx..x.x",hh:"...x.....x..",kk:"g.....g....."}},note:"Swung ride, hi-hat on 2 and 4, a feathered kick on 1 and 3. Light enough for the head."},{type:"groove",groove:{id:"modal-jazz-swing",name:"Modal swing with comping",role:"Chorus",steps:12,beats:4,meter:"4/4",t:{rd:"x..x.xx..x.x",hh:"...x.....x..",sn:"..g..g..x..g",kk:"g..g..g..g.."}},note:"Snare comping on the swung upbeats and a feathered kick on all four beats, for the solo sections."},{type:"tab",id:"modal-jazz-quartal-comp",title:"Quartal comping on a Dorian vamp",instr:"guitar",bpm:132,grid:3,tone:"modal-jazz-clean",notes:[[0,5,5,4,"D"],[0,4,5,4,"D"],[0,3,5,4,"D"],[0,2,6,4,"D"],[5,5,5,1,"U"],[5,4,5,1,"U"],[5,3,5,1,"U"],[5,2,6,1,"U"],[18,5,7,2,"D"],[18,4,7,2,"D"],[18,3,7,2,"D"],[18,2,8,2,"D"],[21,5,10,3,"D"],[21,4,10,3,"D"],[21,3,10,3,"D"],[21,2,12,3,"D"],[24,5,12,4,"D,acc"],[24,4,12,4,"D,acc"],[24,3,12,4,"D,acc"],[24,2,13,4,"D,acc"],[29,5,12,1,"U"],[29,4,12,1,"U"],[29,3,12,1,"U"],[29,2,13,1,"U"],[36,5,3,2,"D"],[36,4,3,2,"D"],[36,3,4,2,"D"],[36,2,5,2,"D"],[39,5,5,9,"D"],[39,4,5,9,"D"],[39,3,5,9,"D"],[39,2,6,9,"D"]],note:'Shapes built on D, E, G, A and C, all stacked in fourths and all inside D Dorian. Bars 1 and 3 use the Charleston rhythm: beat 1 and the "and" of 2. Bar 2 climbs on beats 3 and 4, and bar 4 steps up from the C shape into D.'},{type:"technique",id:"walking-bass",note:"Over one long chord the bassist has to invent the shape of each bar. Try walking D Dorian yourself to feel how the line drives the vamp."}]},{id:"lines",title:"Improvising over one chord for a long time",body:"Sixteen bars on one chord can feel like open water. The players who swim well there think in phrases.\n\n- **Start with a motif.** Three or four notes with a clear rhythm. Play it, repeat it, move it to a different beat, shift it up the mode, then stretch its ending. Jim Hall was known for building solos out of motifs this way.\n- **Use silence.** Silence is as much a part of Hall's music as sound. A rest after a phrase lets the band answer you.\n- **Feature the color note.** In D Dorian land on B; over Fmaj7 in F Lydian, land on B too. Those notes tell the listener which mode they are in.\n- **Think like a horn.** Grant Green avoided chordal playing for single-note lines and said he listened to horn players such as Charlie Parker and Miles Davis. Breathe between ideas.\n\nThe first line below states a motif, displaces it by a beat, moves it up a step and resolves to D. The second floats over a Lydian vamp and ends on the raised fourth.",cites:["m6","m13","m16","m18"],blocks:[{type:"tab",id:"modal-jazz-motif-line",title:"One motif, four bars (D Dorian)",instr:"guitar",bpm:132,grid:3,tone:"modal-jazz-clean",notes:[[0,1,5,2,"D"],[2,1,8,1,"U"],[3,1,7,5,"p,v"],[15,1,5,2,"D"],[17,1,8,1,"U"],[18,1,7,3,"p"],[21,2,8,3,"D"],[24,1,7,2,"D"],[26,1,10,1,"U"],[27,1,8,2,"p"],[29,1,5,1,"p"],[30,1,7,2,"h"],[32,2,8,1,"U"],[33,1,5,3,"D"],[38,2,6,1,"U"],[39,2,5,2,"p"],[41,3,7,7,"D,v"]],note:"Bar 1 states A, C, B (the B is Dorian's sixth). Bar 2 repeats it a beat later. Bar 3 moves it up a step and extends it. Bar 4 resolves to D after a rest."},{type:"tab",id:"modal-jazz-lydian-line",title:"Floating over Fmaj7#11",instr:"guitar",bpm:132,grid:3,tone:"modal-jazz-clean",notes:[[0,2,6,2,"D"],[2,1,5,1,"U"],[3,1,7,2,"D,acc"],[5,1,10,1,"U"],[6,1,12,3,"s"],[9,1,10,2,"s"],[11,1,7,1,"U"],[12,1,8,2,"D"],[14,1,7,1,"p"],[15,1,5,2,"p"],[17,2,8,1,"U"],[18,1,7,6,"D,v"]],note:"F Lydian: the accented B in bar 1 and the held B at the end are the raised fourth. Let the last note ring over the vamp."},{type:"scale",root:"F",scale:"lydian",view:"box",note:"Same notes as D Dorian, different home. Hear how B changes job when F is in the bass."},{type:"technique",id:"alternate-picking",note:"Keep the pick moving down and up with the swung eighths; slurs (the p and h marks) soften the line like a horn."}]},{id:"tone",title:"Tone: warm, round and lightly amplified",body:"The traditional jazz guitar sound comes from an archtop with a broad hollow body and humbucking pickups, strung with heavy flatwound strings and played clean, often through a tube amp and without effects. Reverb has long been part of the picture.\n\nJim Hall's sound has been called mellow and his technique subtle. That is a good target for modal playing: the fourth-stacked voicings stay clear, and a single line has room to breathe. Set the amp so a five-note So What chord rings without any fuzz, and loud enough that you can still play softly.\n\nSince the 1970s some jazz guitarists have added overdrive, chorus or wah, so a touch of extra gain for solos sits within the tradition if your own music leans that way. Start clean, and add it last.",cites:["m9","m16"],blocks:[{type:"tone",id:"modal-jazz-clean",name:"Warm archtop clean",kind:"electric",guitar:"Hollow-body archtop (ES-175 or L-5 type) or a semi-hollow",pickups:"Neck humbucker",strings:"Flatwound 12s or 13s",amp:"Clean tube combo",settings:{gain:2,bass:5,mid:6,treble:4,presence:3,reverb:3},pedals:["reverb"],demo:"modal-jazz-motif-line",tips:["An archtop or semi-hollow with a humbucker gets you close. Traditional players also prefer heavy flatwound strings.","Keep the gain low enough that a five-note quartal chord stays clear from the bottom note to the top.","Use reverb as a small room around the guitar. It has long been part of the jazz guitar sound.","Keep the amp modest and let your picking set the dynamics. Jim Hall's sound has been called mellow and his technique subtle, and that is the target here."],cites:["m9","m16"],note:"Play the motif line through this card, then try the comping tab: the voicings should stay clear at every setting you choose."}]},{id:"form",title:"Form: AABA on two modes",body:'The classic modal head is a 32-bar AABA. "So What" and Coltrane\'s "Impressions" both put D Dorian in the A sections and lift to Eb Dorian for the B section, so the whole form runs on two chords. The melody of the head does the talking, and the solos keep the same map.\n\nThe map below uses that shape with one change for your own tune: the bridge opens on a Lydian chord, Bbmaj7#11, for four bars before the half-step lift to Ebm11, then drops back to Dm11. Play the head in the lighter two-feel, then open into the swing groove for a full 32-bar solo chorus.',cites:["m1","m13","m14"],blocks:[{type:"form",name:"Modal AABA map",sections:[{type:"intro",bars:4,chords:"i11 -",groove:"modal-jazz-two",fill:""},{type:"verse",name:"Head A",bars:8,chords:"i11",groove:"modal-jazz-two",fill:""},{type:"verse",name:"Head A",bars:8,chords:"i11",groove:"modal-jazz-two",fill:"f-snare8"},{type:"bridge",name:"Head B",bars:8,chords:"bVImaj7#11 - - - bii11 - - -",groove:"modal-jazz-two",fill:"f-snare8"},{type:"verse",name:"Head A",bars:8,chords:"i11",groove:"modal-jazz-two",fill:"f-build"},{type:"solo",name:"Solo chorus",bars:32,chords:"i11 - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - bVImaj7#11 - - - bii11 - - - i11 - - - - - - -",groove:"modal-jazz-swing",fill:"f-snare8"},{type:"outro",name:"Head out",bars:8,chords:"i11",groove:"modal-jazz-two",fill:"f-stop"}],note:"Write an eight-bar head melody for the A sections with one clear motif, and a contrasting phrase for the bridge."}]},{id:"together",title:"Put it together",body:"Start with the vamp. Loop eight bars of Dm11 with the two-feel groove and comp with the fourth-stacked shapes until you can move between them without looking. Then write your head: a short motif for the A sections and an answer for the bridge.\n\nFor the solo, give yourself rules for the first few choruses: one motif per eight bars, at least one bar of silence, and a landing on the color note at the end of each phrase. Record yourself and listen for the moments that breathe. Those are usually the best ones.",cites:["m1","m6","m13","m16"],blocks:[{type:"checklist",items:["Your head sits on one or two modes with long stretches of each chord.","You can name the color note of each mode (B in D Dorian, B in F Lydian) and land on it.","Your comping moves fourth-stacked shapes inside the mode and leaves space.","You can build a chorus from one motif: state, repeat, displace, move it up, resolve.","You leave at least one bar of silence in every eight-bar phrase.","Your tone is clean enough that a So What chord rings clearly."]}]}]}),LEARN.course({id:"jazz-fusion",family:"Jazz",name:"Jazz fusion",tagline:"Jazz harmony and improvising at rock volume: odd meters, funk grooves, Lydian colors and singing legato leads.",level:"Advanced",era:"Late 1960s to today",artists:["Mahavishnu Orchestra","Return to Forever","Weather Report","Tony Williams Lifetime","John McLaughlin","Allan Holdsworth","Pat Metheny","Mike Stern"],listen:["Miles Davis – Bitches Brew (album)","Tony Williams Lifetime – Emergency! (album)","Mahavishnu Orchestra – The Inner Mounting Flame (album)","Mahavishnu Orchestra – Birds of Fire (album)","Tony Williams – Believe It (album, with Allan Holdsworth)","Pat Metheny – Bright Size Life (album)"],key:"E minor",bpm:116,meter:"4/4",techniques:["legato","alternate-picking","vibrato","palm-muting"],sources:[{id:"f1",title:"Jazz fusion",site:"Wikipedia",url:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jazz_fusion",kind:"encyclopedia"},{id:"f3",title:"John McLaughlin (musician)",site:"Wikipedia",url:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_McLaughlin_(musician)",kind:"encyclopedia"},{id:"f4",title:"Allan Holdsworth",site:"Wikipedia",url:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Allan_Holdsworth",kind:"encyclopedia"},{id:"f6",title:"Pat Metheny: The Jazz Guitar Prodigy at 60",author:"Dan Forte",site:"Vintage Guitar",year:2016,url:"https://www.vintageguitar.com/26318/pat-metheny-2/",kind:"interview"},{id:"f7",title:"Scott Henderson Interview: Effected by Fusion",site:"Guitar.com",year:2015,url:"https://guitar.com/features/scott-henderson-interview-effected-by-fusion/",kind:"interview"},{id:"f13",title:"Mike Stern: Mixed Sources",author:"Oscar Jordan",site:"Vintage Guitar",year:2015,url:"https://www.vintageguitar.com/22141/mike-stern/",kind:"interview"},{id:"f14",title:"The Signature Sound of Mike Stern",author:"Phil Gates",site:"BOSS Users Group",url:"https://www.boss.info/us/community/boss_users_group/815/",kind:"interview"},{id:"f15",title:'Mike Stern\'s Epic "Original Rays" Solo',author:"Jeff Jacobson",site:"Premier Guitar",year:2023,url:"https://www.premierguitar.com/lessons/intermediate/mike-stern-original-rays-solo?rebelltitem=6",kind:"lesson"},{id:"f16",title:"What Makes the Melodic Minor Scale So... Melodic?",author:"Pete Weise",site:"Premier Guitar",year:2021,url:"https://www.premierguitar.com/articles/31226-what-makes-the-melodic-minor-scale-so-melodic",kind:"lesson"},{id:"f17",title:"The Lydian Flat-7 Scale",site:"Premier Guitar",year:2010,url:"https://www.premierguitar.com/lessons/the-lydian-flat-7-scale",kind:"lesson"},{id:"f18",title:"Allan Holdsworth: the reluctant guitarist, in conversation with Mark Gilbert (JJ 05/92)",author:"Mark Gilbert",site:"Jazz Journal",year:1992,url:"https://jazzjournal.co.uk/2022/05/31/jj-05-92-allan-holdsworth-the-reluctant-guitarist-in-conversation-with-mark-gilbert/",kind:"interview"},{id:"f19",title:"The Ultimate Guide to Guitar Effects Pedal Order and Signal Chain",author:"Henry Yates",site:"BOSS Articles",url:"https://articles.boss.info/the-ultimate-guide-to-guitar-effects-pedal-order-and-signal-chain/",kind:"article"},{id:"f20",title:"Electric Etudes: John McLaughlin",author:"Shawn Persinger",site:"Premier Guitar",year:2015,url:"https://www.premierguitar.com/articles/23449-electric-etude-john-mclaughlin",kind:"lesson"},{id:"f21",title:"Gary Willis: No Borders, No Limits",author:"Anil Prasad",site:"Innerviews",year:1997,url:"https://www.innerviews.org/inner/willis-1",kind:"interview"}],study:[{title:"Emergency!",kind:"album",author:"Tony Williams Lifetime (1969)",note:"Rock intensity meets jazz spontaneity, with McLaughlin on guitar, recorded just before Bitches Brew."},{title:"The Inner Mounting Flame",kind:"album",author:"Mahavishnu Orchestra (1971)",note:"The template for odd meters, Indian influence and high-energy alternate picking."},{title:"Believe It",kind:"album",author:"The Tony Williams Lifetime (1975)",note:"Allan Holdsworth's legato in a funk-rock fusion band; study the phrasing more than the speed."},{title:"Bright Size Life",kind:"album",author:"Pat Metheny (1976)",url:"https://ecmrecords.com/product/bright-size-life-pat-metheny/",note:"A trio with Jaco Pastorius and Bob Moses: the clean, open side of fusion guitar."},{title:"Jazzology: The Encyclopedia of Jazz Theory for All Musicians",kind:"book",author:"Robert Rawlins and Nor Eddine Bahha (Hal Leonard, 2005)",url:"https://www.halleonard.com/product/311167/jazzology",note:"A clear reference for melodic-minor modes, Lydian dominant and the altered scale."},{title:"Innerviews: Gary Willis, No Borders, No Limits",kind:"interview",author:"Anil Prasad (1997)",url:"https://www.innerviews.org/inner/willis-1",note:"A long-form interview with the bassist who co-leads Tribal Tech with Scott Henderson, on how a working fusion band shapes its material."}],steps:[{id:"sound",title:"The sound: jazz at rock volume",body:"Jazz fusion took shape in the late 1960s, when musicians combined jazz harmony and improvisation with rock, funk and R&B. Miles Davis's *In a Silent Way* (1969) is considered his first fusion album, and *Bitches Brew* moved further toward rock backbeats and bass grooves. Tony Williams Lifetime, with John McLaughlin on guitar, joined rock intensity to jazz spontaneity on *Emergency!*, recorded three months before *Bitches Brew*.\n\nThe 1970s bands set the template. McLaughlin's Mahavishnu Orchestra, whose first lineup ran from 1971 to 1973, drew on rock and Indian classical music and played in complex time signatures. Return to Forever blended jazz, rock, pop and Brazilian music, and Weather Report ran from 1970 to 1986. Electric guitar, bass, electric piano and synthesizers took over from the acoustic rhythm section, and improvised solos stayed at the center.\n\nGuitarists found different voices: McLaughlin's fast alternate picking, Allan Holdsworth's horn-like legato, Pat Metheny's wide, clean tone spread across several amps, and the chorused sound of Mike Stern, who made his name with Blood, Sweat & Tears, Jaco Pastorius and Miles Davis. Scott Henderson grew up on the rock, blues, funk and soul of the 1960s and '70s, and since the mid-1980s he has co-led the fusion quartet Tribal Tech with bassist Gary Willis.",cites:["f1","f3","f6","f7","f13","f18","f20","f21"],blocks:[{type:"callout",tone:"history",text:"Fusion grew louder and more virtuosic as it went: the genre emphasized exploration, energy, intensity and volume, and its later branches include jazz-funk and smooth jazz.",cites:["f1"]}]},{id:"harmony",title:"Harmony: vamps, Lydian colors and slash chords",body:"Fusion arrangements run from simple groove vamps to complex progressions with odd meters and counter-melodies. The vamp is the place to start: one or two chords, colored with extensions, so the band can groove and the soloist can stretch.\n\nThree scale colors do most of the work. **Lydian** is the major scale with a raised fourth, for maj7#11 chords. **Lydian dominant** adds a flat seventh: it is the fourth mode of melodic minor, maps onto dominant chords with a #11, and can replace Mixolydian over a dominant. **Altered**, the seventh mode of melodic minor, puts b9, #9, b5 and #5 on a dominant for extra tension. Holdsworth's vocabulary drew on Lydian, diminished, whole-tone and altered scales, among others.\n\nA handy way to voice these colors on guitar is a triad over a different bass note. A D triad over C gives a C Lydian sound; a D triad over E gives an E9sus4 sound that sits well on an Em9 vamp. Fretkit's progressions have one chord per bar and no slash chords, so the notes tell you what to keep in the bass.",cites:["f1","f4","f16","f17"],blocks:[{type:"progression",name:"Minor vamp with a Lydian lift",chords:"i9 - bVImaj7#11 -",note:"Em9 for two bars, then Cmaj7#11. Keep C in the bass and play a D triad on top (D over C) for the Lydian sound.",cites:["f16"]},{type:"progression",name:"Lydian dominant to altered",chords:"IV9 - bVII9 V7#9",note:"A9 and D9 take a #11 (Lydian dominant). B7#9 takes the altered scale and pulls back to Em9. Bass: A, D, B.",cites:["f16","f17"]},{type:"shapes",title:"Fusion voicings in E minor",shapes:[{name:"Em9",frets:"0 2 0 0 0 2"},{name:"D over E (E9sus4)",frets:"0 x 0 2 3 2"},{name:"Cmaj7#11",frets:"x 3 2 4 x 2"},{name:"D over C",frets:"x 3 x 2 3 2"},{name:"A13#11",frets:"x 0 5 6 4 2"},{name:"D9",frets:"x 5 4 5 5 5"},{name:"B7#9",frets:"x 2 1 2 3 x"},{name:"Fmaj7#11",frets:"x 8 7 9 x 7"}],note:'The "over" shapes are triads above a bass note. Play the bass with your thumb or let the bassist take it.'},{type:"tab",id:"jazz-fusion-slash-voicings",title:"Slash-chord colors, clean",instr:"guitar",bpm:76,grid:2,ring:!0,tone:"jazz-fusion-clean",notes:[[0,6,0,8,""],[2,4,0,6,""],[2,3,2,6,""],[2,2,3,6,""],[5,1,2,3,""],[8,5,3,8,""],[10,3,2,6,""],[10,2,3,6,""],[13,1,2,3,""],[16,5,0,8,""],[18,4,5,6,""],[18,3,6,6,""],[18,2,4,6,""],[21,1,2,3,""],[24,5,2,8,""],[26,4,1,6,""],[26,3,2,6,""],[26,2,3,6,""],[29,1,3,3,"v"]],note:"D over E, D over C, A13#11, B7#9 with a #5 on top. Bass note first, then the triad, then the top note, all ringing."}]},{id:"rhythm",title:"Grooves: odd meters and funk",body:"Fusion draws on rock backbeats and funk grooves, and its arrangements often use unconventional time signatures. The Mahavishnu Orchestra's sound included complex time signatures, and McLaughlin's playing uses non-Western scales and odd meters shaped by Indian music.\n\nThe easiest way into an odd meter is to group it. A bar of 7/8 is seven eighth notes. Count it 1-2, 1-2, 1-2-3 and let the kick and snare mark the groups. Once the count lives in your body, write riffs that accent the same groups.\n\nFor contrast, a sixteenth-note funk groove in 4/4 gives the chorus a deep pocket, and a half-time ride feel opens up the bridge. The riff below stays in 7/8: two bars on E with a palm-muted low string, then two on C, each pair ending with a chord stab on the long group.",cites:["f1","f3","f20"],blocks:[{type:"groove",groove:{id:"jazz-fusion-seven",name:"Fusion 7/8 (2+2+3)",role:"Verse",steps:14,beats:3.5,meter:"7/8",t:{hh:"x.x.x.x.x.x.x.",sn:"....x...g...X.",kk:"x.....x.x....x"}},note:"Eighth notes on the hat. The kick starts each group of two, the snare cracks on the second group and on the last eighth, and a pickup kick pulls you back to one."},{type:"groove",groove:{id:"jazz-fusion-funk",name:"Fusion funk 4/4",role:"Chorus",steps:16,beats:4,meter:"4/4",swing:0,t:{hh:"x.x.x.x.x.x.x...",oh:"..............x.",sn:"....X..g.g..X..g",kk:"x..x..x...x..x.."}},note:"Straight sixteenths with ghost notes and an open hat at the end of the bar."},{type:"groove",groove:{id:"jazz-fusion-halftime",name:"Half-time ride",role:"Bridge & feels",steps:16,beats:4,meter:"4/4",swing:0,t:{rd:"x.x.x.x.x.x.x.x.",sn:"........X.......",kk:"x.....x...x....."}},note:"Snare on 3 only: the floor drops out from under the soloist."},{type:"tab",id:"jazz-fusion-seven-riff",title:"7/8 riff on E and C",instr:"guitar",bpm:116,grid:2,tone:"jazz-fusion-lead",notes:[[0,6,0,1,"pm,acc"],[1,6,0,1,"pm"],[2,6,3,1,"acc"],[3,6,0,1,"pm"],[4,5,0,1,"acc"],[5,5,2,1,""],[6,4,0,1,""],[7,6,0,1,"pm,acc"],[8,6,0,1,"pm"],[9,6,3,1,"acc"],[10,6,0,1,"pm"],[11,4,0,2,"acc"],[11,3,0,2,"acc"],[11,2,0,2,"acc"],[11,1,2,2,"acc"],[13,5,2,1,""],[14,5,3,1,"pm,acc"],[15,5,3,1,"pm"],[16,4,2,1,"acc"],[17,5,3,1,"pm"],[18,4,4,1,"acc"],[19,4,5,1,""],[20,3,4,1,""],[21,5,3,1,"pm,acc"],[22,5,3,1,"pm"],[23,4,2,1,"acc"],[24,5,3,1,"pm"],[25,5,3,2,"acc"],[25,4,2,2,"acc"],[25,3,4,2,"acc"],[25,1,2,2,"acc"],[27,5,2,1,""]],note:"Four bars of 7/8, counted 1-2, 1-2, 1-2-3 (accents mark each group). Bars 1 and 2 use E minor pentatonic; bars 3 and 4 outline C Lydian with the F#."},{type:"technique",id:"palm-muting",note:"Mute the pedal notes and let the accented notes ring open; the contrast is what makes the meter audible."}]},{id:"lines",title:"Lines: legato, picking and altered tension",body:"Fusion soloing grew from two right-hand approaches. McLaughlin is known for fast, precise alternate picking. Holdsworth always wanted to play like a horn, and he worked until his picked and hammered notes were hard to tell apart: long phrases of slurred notes with few picked ones.\n\nPractice both. Alternate picking gives you attack and drive; legato gives you the smooth, horn-like line. A useful middle ground is to pick the first note on each string and slur the rest.\n\nNote choice matters as much as the hands. Over a maj7#11 chord, lean on the raised fourth. Over a dominant with a #11, use Lydian dominant. Over an altered dominant, run the altered scale for tension, then resolve each tension note by a half step into the next chord: over B7alt going to Em9, C falls to B and F falls to E.",cites:["f3","f4","f16","f18"],blocks:[{type:"tab",id:"jazz-fusion-legato-lydian",title:"Legato run in C Lydian",instr:"guitar",bpm:100,grid:4,tone:"jazz-fusion-lead",notes:[[0,4,4,1,"D"],[1,4,5,1,"h"],[2,4,7,1,"h"],[3,3,4,1,"D"],[4,3,5,1,"h"],[5,3,7,1,"h"],[6,2,5,1,"D"],[7,2,7,1,"h"],[8,2,8,1,"h"],[9,1,5,1,"D"],[10,1,7,1,"h"],[11,1,8,1,"h"],[12,1,7,1,"p"],[13,1,5,1,"p"],[14,2,8,1,"U"],[15,2,7,1,"p"],[16,2,5,1,"p"],[17,3,7,1,"U"],[18,3,5,1,"p"],[19,3,4,1,"p"],[20,2,7,10,"U,v"]],note:"Three notes per string over Cmaj7#11: one pick stroke per string, the rest hammer-ons and pull-offs. It ends on F#, the raised fourth. Aim for every note at the same volume."},{type:"tab",id:"jazz-fusion-altered-line",title:"Altered tension into Em9",instr:"guitar",bpm:92,grid:4,tone:"jazz-fusion-lead",notes:[[0,1,5,1,"D"],[1,2,8,1,"U"],[2,2,6,1,"D"],[3,2,4,1,"U"],[4,3,7,1,"D"],[5,3,5,1,"U"],[6,3,4,1,"D"],[7,3,5,1,"U"],[8,3,7,1,"D"],[9,2,4,1,"U"],[10,2,6,1,"D"],[11,2,8,1,"U"],[12,1,5,1,"D"],[13,1,8,3,"U"],[16,1,7,2,"D,v"],[18,2,8,1,"D"],[19,2,7,1,"U"],[20,2,5,2,"D"],[22,3,7,1,"D"],[23,2,5,1,"U"],[24,2,7,8,"D,v"]],note:"Bar 1 is B altered (B, C, D, D#, F, G, A) over B7#9. The high C (the flat nine) falls a half step to B as Em9 arrives, and the line settles on F#, the ninth."},{type:"scale",root:"C",scale:"lydian",view:"3nps",note:"The three-notes-per-string layout the legato run uses."},{type:"scale",root:"A",scale:"lydDom",view:"3nps",note:"For A9 with a #11 in the chorus progression."},{type:"scale",root:"B",scale:"altered",view:"3nps",note:"For B7#9 before it resolves to Em9."},{type:"technique",id:"legato",note:"Fusion legato is about evenness: the hammered note should match the picked one."},{type:"technique",id:"alternate-picking",note:"The altered line is marked strictly down-up; keep the pick moving through the string changes."},{type:"technique",id:"vibrato",note:"A slow, wide vibrato on the last note makes a distorted line sing like a horn."}]},{id:"tone",title:"Tone: a singing lead and a wide clean",body:"Fusion players often carry two very different sounds. The lead tone sustains like a horn. Mike Stern's documented rig is a good model: a Fender '65 reissue Twin Reverb, the Boss DS-1 distortion he says he has used on every one of his own records, an old Yamaha SPX90 for chorus, and two Boss DD-3 delays, one on all the time and one set long for occasional use. Scott Henderson uses one distortion pedal at a time and never combines them, because together they make too much noise.\n\n**Pedal order** shapes the result. A compressor usually goes near the start, close to the guitar, so it works on an even clean signal. Drive comes next, then modulation such as chorus, with delay and reverb last so the repeats stay clean. Use only as much gain as it takes for a hammer-on to match a picked note. Holdsworth liked Marshalls for single-note soloing but not for chords, so keep a cleaner setting for comping.\n\nThe clean sound is wide and soft. Pat Metheny says he can't stand chorus-type effects; he spreads delays across several amps instead, and points out that you don't have to play loud for a full sound.",cites:["f4","f6","f7","f13","f14","f15","f19"],blocks:[{type:"tone",id:"jazz-fusion-lead",name:"Singing fusion lead",kind:"electric",guitar:"Solid-body or semi-hollow with humbuckers",pickups:"Neck humbucker for legato, bridge for bite",strings:"10s",amp:"Loud, clean tube amp with headroom (Fender Twin type), or two in stereo",settings:{gain:6,bass:5,mid:7,treble:6,presence:5,reverb:3,delay:4,master:6},pedals:["comp","dist","chorus","delay","reverb"],demo:"jazz-fusion-legato-lydian",tips:["Run a compressor first, close to the guitar, then one drive pedal into a clean amp, chorus after the drive, and delay and reverb last.","Set the drive just high enough that a hammer-on from nowhere sounds as loud as a picked note. Holdsworth wanted the two to be hard to tell apart.","Use one distortion pedal at a time. Scott Henderson never stacks his because of the noise; his favorite pedal is an RC Booster.","For a Mike Stern-style lead: a Fender Twin Reverb, a Boss DS-1, a Yamaha SPX90 for chorus, and two Boss DD-3 delays, one always on and one set long.","A compressor ahead of the drive works on the clean signal and keeps the level even, which helps legato lines.","Keep a cleaner setting for chords. Holdsworth liked Marshalls for single-note soloing but not for chords."],cites:["f4","f7","f13","f14","f15","f18","f19"],note:"Try the legato run with the gain at 3, then at 8. The best setting is the lowest one where the slurred notes still speak."},{type:"tone",id:"jazz-fusion-clean",name:"Wide clean with delay",kind:"electric",guitar:"Hollow-body or semi-hollow archtop",pickups:"Neck humbucker",strings:"11s or 12s",amp:"Clean amp with plenty of headroom; two or three amps in stereo if you have them",settings:{gain:1,bass:5,mid:5,treble:5,presence:4,reverb:4,delay:5},pedals:["comp","volume","delay","reverb"],demo:"jazz-fusion-slash-voicings",tips:["Pat Metheny spreads his signal across several amps with slightly different sounds and delays, and says you do not have to play loud to get a full sound.","Get the width from delay rather than chorus. Metheny says he can't stand chorus-type effects.","Keep it clean. Metheny says his sound has always come from a solid-state sound, without the slight fuzz of a Vox AC30 or Fender Twin.","Use a volume pedal to remove the pick attack so chords swell in. Holdsworth used swells with delay and chorus to sustain chords like a horn section."],cites:["f4","f6","f15"],note:"Play the slash-chord tab through this card and raise the delay until the chords overlap without blurring."}]},{id:"form",title:"Form: heads, vamps and solo sections",body:"A simple way to shape a fusion tune is a composed head around open solo sections. The arrangement can be a simple groove vamp or a complex progression with unconventional meters and counter-melodies, and the solos sit at the center, often of variable length. Bands strike the balance differently: Gary Willis has said Tribal Tech first wrote tunes strong enough to work on a good or a bad night for the soloists, and later moved toward open improvisation.\n\nThe map below mixes meters on purpose. The intro and verses sit in 7/8 on the Em9 to Cmaj7#11 vamp. The chorus snaps into a 4/4 funk groove over the Lydian dominant turnaround. The solo section returns to the vamp in 4/4 so the soloist can stretch, the bridge drops to a half-time ride feel with two Lydian chords a fourth apart, and the head comes back in 7/8.\n\nWrite the head melody first, over the 7/8 verse. A short, rhythmic phrase that accents the 2+2+3 grouping will make the odd meter feel natural to a listener.",cites:["f1","f21"],blocks:[{type:"progression",name:"Bridge: Lydian chords",chords:"bIImaj7#11 - bVImaj7#11 -",note:"Fmaj7#11 to Cmaj7#11, the same voicing moved down a fourth. Keep the bass on F and C so each Lydian color sounds clearly."},{type:"form",name:"Fusion song map",sections:[{type:"intro",bars:4,chords:"i9 - - -",groove:"jazz-fusion-seven",fill:""},{type:"verse",name:"Head (7/8)",bars:8,chords:"i9 - bVImaj7#11 -",groove:"jazz-fusion-seven",fill:"f-snare16"},{type:"chorus",bars:8,chords:"IV9 - bVII9 V7#9",groove:"jazz-fusion-funk",fill:"f-toms"},{type:"solo",name:"Solo vamp",bars:16,chords:"i9 - - - bVImaj7#11 - - -",groove:"jazz-fusion-funk",fill:"f-build"},{type:"bridge",bars:8,chords:"bIImaj7#11 - bVImaj7#11 -",groove:"jazz-fusion-halftime",fill:"f-snare8"},{type:"verse",name:"Head (7/8)",bars:8,chords:"i9 - bVImaj7#11 -",groove:"jazz-fusion-seven",fill:"f-snare16"},{type:"chorus",bars:8,chords:"IV9 - bVII9 V7#9",groove:"jazz-fusion-funk",fill:"f-toms"},{type:"outro",bars:4,chords:"i9 - - -",groove:"jazz-fusion-seven",fill:"f-stop"}],note:"Change the solo length to suit your band; fusion solos are often open-ended."}]},{id:"together",title:"Put it together",body:"Start with the groove. Loop the 7/8 drums and count the groups out loud while you play the riff, until the accents land without thinking. Then load the song map and play the vamp, the funk chorus and the bridge in order, switching between your clean and lead sounds.\n\nFor the solo, set yourself a plan: legato over the Lydian vamp, picked lines over the funk, altered tension on B7#9 resolving cleanly into Em9. Record it, then check the hard parts at half speed: the meter changes and the resolutions are where fusion either locks in or falls apart.",cites:["f1","f4","f16"],blocks:[{type:"checklist",items:["You can count and play the 7/8 groove as 2+2+3 without losing beat one.","Your vamp has a Lydian or Lydian dominant color, voiced as a triad over a bass note.","You can play the altered line and resolve its tension notes by half step.","Your legato phrase sounds as even as your picked phrase.","Your lead tone sustains with only as much gain as it needs, and your clean tone is wide and soft.","Your song map has a composed head, an open solo section and at least one change of meter."]}]}]});